---
/**
 * About — static page. Honest description of the product and its shipped
 * scope. No invented stats, team, or history.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedLinks from '../../components/content/RelatedLinks.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="What the Graphing Calculator is: features, how the math engine works, and how your data is handled."
  canonicalPath="/about/"
  mainClass="mx-auto w-full max-w-3xl px-4 py-10 sm:py-14"
>
  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900 dark:text-white">About</h1>

  <div class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
    <p>
      The Graphing Calculator is an original, browser-based math tool for graphing functions and
      exploring mathematics. It is an independent implementation — built from scratch, not derived
      from any other graphing product.
    </p>
    <p>
      What it does today: plot Cartesian functions, parametric curves, polar equations, and
      inequalities on an interactive canvas graph with zoom, pan, and touch support; analyze
      expressions with real numerical methods (roots via Brent&rsquo;s method, derivatives via
      central differences, integrals via adaptive Simpson&rsquo;s rule, tables, tangent lines);
      animate parameters with variables and sliders; get plain-English help from an AI assistant
      that issues calculator commands while the math engine does the actual computing; and save,
      share, import, and export graph states.
    </p>
    <h2 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">About the creator</h2>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-start gap-5 sm:flex-row sm:items-center">
      <img
        src="/images/firoz-khan.webp"
        alt="Firoz Khan, creator of Graphing Calculator"
        width="160"
        height="160"
        loading="lazy"
        class="h-32 w-32 shrink-0 rounded-full object-cover ring-1 ring-slate-200 dark:ring-slate-700"
      />
      <div>
        <p class="font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Firoz Khan</p>
        <p class="mt-1">
          Graphing Calculator is built and maintained by Firoz Khan. Connect with him here:
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
          <li>
            <a
              href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/firoz-khan-1153358a/"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class="text-brand-700 hover:underline dark:text-brand-300"
            >
              LinkedIn
            </a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a
              href="https://github.com/fkdigitalmedia"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class="text-brand-700 hover:underline dark:text-brand-300"
            >
              GitHub
            </a>
          </li>
          <li>
            <a
              href="https://www.instagram.com/rtibyfiroz/"
              target="_blank"
              rel="noopener noreferrer"
              class="text-brand-700 hover:underline dark:text-brand-300"
            >
              Instagram
            </a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <h2 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
      How your data is handled
    </h2>
    <p>
      Everything runs on your device. Graphs you save are stored in your browser&rsquo;s local
      storage; share links encode the graph state in the URL itself. There are no accounts, and no
      personal data is sent to a server by the calculator. The site uses Google Analytics to measure
      aggregate usage (see the privacy policy). AI assistant requests go to the configured AI
      provider only when you use the assistant.
    </p>
    <h2 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">Honesty about results</h2>
    <p>
      Numerical methods are approximations, and the calculator says so where it matters: when a
      root, derivative, or integral cannot be computed — a corner, a jump, a singularity — the tool
      reports that honestly instead of returning a misleading number.
    </p>
    <h2 class="pt-2 text-xl font-semibold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">How we review content</h2>
    <p>
      Every guide on this site is checked for mathematical accuracy before it is published. The
      mathematical facts in each guide — roots, domains, example values — are verified against the
      site&rsquo;s own expression engine, the same code that powers the calculator, so what you read
      matches what the tool computes.
    </p>
    <p>
      A human reviewer on the Graphing Calculator team then reads each guide for clarity and
      correctness. When an error is found — a wrong sign, a misleading example, a step that skips
      too much — it is corrected before the guide goes live, and the guide carries a &ldquo;Last
      reviewed&rdquo; date so you can see when that check happened.
    </p>
  </div>

  <RelatedLinks paths={['/graphing-calculator/', '/math-functions/', '/learn/', '/examples/']} />
</BaseLayout>
